WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.212
A behavior is something an animal does.

00:00:03.213 --> 00:00:07.125
Making a sound can be a behavior that communicates a message.

00:00:07.125 --> 00:00:11.350
Dolphins use sounds to communicate, including with their young.

00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:15.412
Some animals also use sounds to warn their babies about danger.

00:00:15.412 --> 00:00:18.262
Not every sound has the same purpose.

00:00:18.262 --> 00:00:22.825
To explain a behavior, ask what is happening and how it could help the young animal.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.850
Match the four worksheet animals to their examples of care.

00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:30.025
The bird builds a nest for its young.

00:00:30.025 --> 00:00:32.962
The rabbit digs holes that provide shelter.

00:00:32.962 --> 00:00:35.800
The lion protects its cubs from danger.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:39.438
The dolphin uses sounds to communicate with its baby.

00:00:39.438 --> 00:00:42.725
These matches describe the examples in your activity.

00:00:42.725 --> 00:00:46.000
They do not mean an animal can perform only one behavior.

00:00:47.167 --> 00:00:49.904
Can animal parents help their offspring find food?

00:00:49.904 --> 00:00:50.779
Yes.

00:00:50.779 --> 00:00:53.929
Can animals use sounds to warn their babies about danger?

00:00:53.929 --> 00:00:54.792
Yes.

00:00:54.792 --> 00:00:56.692
Now go beyond a yes answer.

00:00:56.692 --> 00:00:59.292
Give an example or explain the purpose.

00:00:59.292 --> 00:01:04.654
A warning can alert a young animal to danger, while bringing food meets hunger.

00:01:04.654 --> 00:01:09.754
An explanation connects the action to a need instead of simply repeating the question.

00:01:10.958 --> 00:01:18.183
Your last worksheet asks you to choose a bird, rabbit, lion or dolphin and draw a parent taking care of its baby.

00:01:18.183 --> 00:01:19.908
Make the behavior visible.

00:01:19.908 --> 00:01:23.958
You might show a bird bringing food or a lion protecting a cub.

00:01:23.958 --> 00:01:27.721
Add a label or a short sentence that explains how the action helps.

00:01:27.721 --> 00:01:32.183
We judge the scientific idea, not how perfectly you can draw an animal.

00:01:33.375 --> 00:01:39.600
You have connected life cycles, parents and offspring, similarities and differences, and animal care.

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.638
Now use those ideas together.

00:01:41.638 --> 00:01:46.388
In the final mission, read each question carefully and look for evidence.

00:01:46.388 --> 00:01:55.413
Remember that a tadpole belongs to the frog cycle, a caterpillar belongs to the butterfly cycle, and different behaviors can help young animals survive.

00:01:55.413 --> 00:01:59.738
You can pause, replay or use a word hint whenever you need one.
